The functional form of the present model was selected to provide a simplified phenomenological description of austenite formation during continuous heating in multicomponent martensitic stainless steels. The dependence of the critical temperatures on alloy chemistry was represented using linear compositional terms, consistent with the commonly adopted additive treatment of alloying element effects in empirical transformation–temperature relationships for steels (Ref  14 ,  15 ,  17 ). The logarithmic heating rate term was introduced to account for the experimentally observed kinetic delay in austenite formation during non-equilibrium heating, which has been widely associated with diffusion-controlled transformation behavior and Kissinger-type rate dependence (Ref  20 ,  22 ,  24 ). Unlike earlier predictive approaches that are primarily composition-based or equilibrium-derived, the present model incorporates heating rate directly into the regression framework and was calibrated using experimentally measured dilatometric transformation data obtained under controlled continuous heating conditions for CA6NM steels. Consequently, the model provides a combined treatment of compositional and kinetic effects within the investigated processing range. Experimental results in this current study indicate that critical transformation temperatures in CA6NM weakly depend on prior austenite grain size in the range of studied  G m \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
